WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.082
Lesson two point six.

2
00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.612
Where the genre drummers' songs come from,

3
00:00:03.612 --> 00:00:04.852
and what was thrown away.

4
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.049
XMIDI is a collection of a hundred and

5
00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:13.201
eight thousand and twenty-three MIDI

6
00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:15.586
files, from a twenty twenty-five paper

7
00:00:15.586 --> 00:00:16.400
called XMusic.

8
00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:19.541
Lesson two point one said why we added it:

9
00:00:19.541 --> 00:00:21.461
every file carries a genre label.

10
00:00:21.943 --> 00:00:23.589
The labels are in the file name.

11
00:00:23.897 --> 00:00:24.823
Here's a real one.

12
00:00:25.132 --> 00:00:27.395
After the word XMIDI comes an emotion:

13
00:00:27.395 --> 00:00:27.703
angry.

14
00:00:28.012 --> 00:00:29.247
Then a genre: rock.

15
00:00:29.555 --> 00:00:31.510
Then an eight-character ID, and the

16
00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:32.024
extension.

17
00:00:32.501 --> 00:00:35.017
There are eleven emotions, and six genres:

18
00:00:35.017 --> 00:00:37.584
rock, pop, country, jazz, classical, and

19
00:00:37.584 --> 00:00:39.345
folk, which the file names spell

20
00:00:39.345 --> 00:00:39.949
traditional.

21
00:00:40.251 --> 00:00:42.415
Rock is the biggest, twenty-six thousand

22
00:00:42.415 --> 00:00:44.528
seven hundred files; traditional the

23
00:00:44.528 --> 00:00:46.491
smallest, three thousand six hundred.

24
00:00:46.793 --> 00:00:48.806
How good these labels are is lesson two

25
00:00:48.806 --> 00:00:49.359
point five.

26
00:00:53.257 --> 00:00:55.905
One script, xmidi count, reads every file

27
00:00:55.905 --> 00:00:57.690
once and writes one row for it.

28
00:00:58.035 --> 00:01:00.453
Here is a real row, for that angry rock

29
00:01:00.453 --> 00:01:00.741
file.

30
00:01:01.255 --> 00:01:03.980
First, the md5 of the file's bytes: a

31
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.978
thirty-two character fingerprint.

32
00:01:06.341 --> 00:01:08.884
Change one byte and it changes completely.

33
00:01:09.248 --> 00:01:11.791
Lakh's files are named by their md5, so

34
00:01:11.791 --> 00:01:14.273
this is how we ask whether a file is one

35
00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:15.242
we already have.

36
00:01:15.762 --> 00:01:17.978
Then the file is parsed with the parser of

37
00:01:17.978 --> 00:01:19.884
our song store, the database every

38
00:01:19.884 --> 00:01:22.099
training song is read from, which lesson

39
00:01:22.099 --> 00:01:23.388
two point eight opens up.

40
00:01:23.697 --> 00:01:25.809
So guitar, drums and bass mean exactly

41
00:01:25.809 --> 00:01:28.334
what they mean for Lakh: the note counts,

42
00:01:28.334 --> 00:01:30.498
the length in seconds and bars, and the

43
00:01:30.498 --> 00:01:32.353
share of the song in four-four time.

44
00:01:32.819 --> 00:01:34.836
From those, usable: yes or no.

45
00:01:35.163 --> 00:01:37.507
The last two fields are the overlap checks

46
00:01:37.507 --> 00:01:39.742
with Lakh, which chapter four explains.

47
00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:42.654
Here is the rule in the code.

48
00:01:42.953 --> 00:01:45.095
At least fifty guitar notes and fifty drum

49
00:01:45.095 --> 00:01:47.287
notes, ninety-five percent of the song in

50
00:01:47.287 --> 00:01:49.528
four-four, eight to a thousand bars, and

51
00:01:49.528 --> 00:01:51.122
twenty seconds to forty minutes.

52
00:01:51.421 --> 00:01:53.613
The numbers are copied from Lakh's chain,

53
00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:55.705
so a usable XMIDI song means exactly what

54
00:01:55.705 --> 00:01:56.950
a usable Lakh song means.

55
00:01:57.249 --> 00:01:59.241
Lesson two point two teaches that chain.

56
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.800
Every file opens: a hundred and eight

57
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.870
thousand and twenty-three, and no parse

58
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:07.314
failures.

59
00:02:07.745 --> 00:02:10.167
The big cut is the first: a guitar part

60
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:11.512
and a drum part together.

61
00:02:11.835 --> 00:02:14.095
Thirty-five thousand files have no guitar

62
00:02:14.095 --> 00:02:16.517
note at all, and fourteen thousand no drum

63
00:02:16.517 --> 00:02:16.786
note.

64
00:02:17.109 --> 00:02:19.423
Asking for fifty of each leaves sixty-five

65
00:02:19.423 --> 00:02:21.092
thousand seven hundred and two.

66
00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.287
The four-four rule removes nothing: every

67
00:02:24.287 --> 00:02:26.604
XMIDI file reads as four-four from start

68
00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:27.000
to end.

69
00:02:27.339 --> 00:02:28.922
The length rule removes ten.

70
00:02:29.261 --> 00:02:31.296
That leaves sixty-five thousand six

71
00:02:31.296 --> 00:02:33.331
hundred and ninety-two usable files.

72
00:02:33.804 --> 00:02:35.879
That's sixty-one percent of the files.

73
00:02:36.207 --> 00:02:37.463
Lakh kept about thirty.

74
00:02:37.791 --> 00:02:40.139
And sixty-two thousand of the usable files

75
00:02:40.139 --> 00:02:41.286
have a bass part too.

76
00:02:44.773 --> 00:02:46.168
Why look for Lakh inside XMIDI?

77
00:02:46.438 --> 00:02:47.338
First, the bridge.

78
00:02:47.608 --> 00:02:49.677
The Lakh transformer, T one twenty-eight,

79
00:02:49.677 --> 00:02:51.522
is scored on XMIDI's validation songs as

80
00:02:51.522 --> 00:02:52.961
the one drummer for every genre.

81
00:02:53.231 --> 00:02:55.256
If one of those songs was in its training,

82
00:02:55.256 --> 00:02:56.920
it would be scored as if it were new.

83
00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.215
Second, the aim is that no song is in both

84
00:02:59.215 --> 00:02:59.575
corpora.

85
00:02:59.844 --> 00:03:01.464
That is achieved only as far as the

86
00:03:01.464 --> 00:03:03.444
fingerprint sees, and only against Lakh's

87
00:03:03.444 --> 00:03:04.073
training side.

88
00:03:04.507 --> 00:03:07.293
The first check is the md5: is this exact

89
00:03:07.293 --> 00:03:08.893
file in Lakh's usable list?

90
00:03:09.249 --> 00:03:09.901
Not one is.

91
00:03:10.363 --> 00:03:12.092
The second check looks for the same tune

92
00:03:12.092 --> 00:03:13.525
under different bytes, with the

93
00:03:13.525 --> 00:03:15.169
fingerprint from lesson two point four.

94
00:03:15.422 --> 00:03:17.362
In short: take the guitar's highest note

95
00:03:17.362 --> 00:03:19.259
at each moment, turn the melody into steps

96
00:03:19.259 --> 00:03:20.903
between notes, hash every run of six

97
00:03:20.903 --> 00:03:22.505
steps, and keep the two hundred and

98
00:03:22.505 --> 00:03:24.191
fifty-six smallest hashes as the song's

99
00:03:24.191 --> 00:03:24.486
sketch.

100
00:03:24.739 --> 00:03:26.341
Two songs are twins if their sketches

101
00:03:26.341 --> 00:03:28.112
share at least thirty-two hashes, and a

102
00:03:28.112 --> 00:03:29.250
Jaccard of at least a half.

103
00:03:29.701 --> 00:03:32.201
Each XMIDI song is checked against Lakh's

104
00:03:32.201 --> 00:03:34.878
training side only, and not its validation

105
00:03:34.878 --> 00:03:35.593
side either.

106
00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:37.794
Lakh's test side is never read.

107
00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.779
Jaccard asks: of all the distinct hashes

108
00:03:41.779 --> 00:03:43.998
in the two sketches, what share do they

109
00:03:43.998 --> 00:03:44.790
have in common?

110
00:03:45.107 --> 00:03:47.536
On the bottom, A's hashes plus B's, minus

111
00:03:47.536 --> 00:03:49.860
the shared ones, which were counted twice.

112
00:03:50.177 --> 00:03:52.553
One means identical; zero means nothing

113
00:03:52.553 --> 00:03:52.923
shared.

114
00:03:53.389 --> 00:03:55.752
Here's a real one: an angry rock file

115
00:03:55.752 --> 00:03:58.005
whose best match in Lakh's training side

116
00:03:58.005 --> 00:04:00.423
shares a hundred and seven hashes, out of

117
00:04:00.423 --> 00:04:02.347
a hundred and seven in each sketch.

118
00:04:02.677 --> 00:04:05.315
Jaccard one: the same set of six-step runs

119
00:04:05.315 --> 00:04:06.249
in both sketches.

120
00:04:06.681 --> 00:04:08.987
In the code: the training song sharing the

121
00:04:08.987 --> 00:04:10.957
most hashes is the best match, Jaccard

122
00:04:10.957 --> 00:04:12.782
over the two sketches, and the rule.

123
00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:14.771
The result.

124
00:04:15.078 --> 00:04:16.969
Twenty-two thousand five hundred and

125
00:04:16.969 --> 00:04:19.321
thirty-four usable files, a third, have a

126
00:04:19.321 --> 00:04:20.804
twin in Lakh's training side.

127
00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:23.411
The rest, forty-three thousand one hundred

128
00:04:23.411 --> 00:04:26.018
and fifty-eight, are clean: usable, not a

129
00:04:26.018 --> 00:04:28.012
Lakh file, and no Lakh training twin.

130
00:04:28.449 --> 00:04:30.279
Thirteen thousand of those twins have

131
00:04:30.279 --> 00:04:31.193
identical sketches.

132
00:04:31.482 --> 00:04:33.696
Rock overlaps most: forty-one percent of

133
00:04:33.696 --> 00:04:34.514
its usable files.

134
00:04:34.803 --> 00:04:36.921
So no file is byte for byte the same, yet

135
00:04:36.921 --> 00:04:38.269
a third carry the same tune.

136
00:04:38.558 --> 00:04:40.531
The XMusic paper says its authors merged

137
00:04:40.531 --> 00:04:42.264
every file's instrument tracks into

138
00:04:42.264 --> 00:04:44.190
seventeen types, which rewrites every

139
00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:44.816
file's bytes.

140
00:04:45.104 --> 00:04:47.271
That may explain it; nobody has checked.

141
00:04:47.819 --> 00:04:50.075
And the fingerprint only sees the guitar's

142
00:04:50.075 --> 00:04:52.435
top line, so an unmeasured number of twins

143
00:04:52.435 --> 00:04:54.534
it can't see are still in the clean set.

144
00:04:58.057 --> 00:04:58.812
One more step.

145
00:04:59.136 --> 00:05:01.347
Forty-three thousand clean files are not

146
00:05:01.347 --> 00:05:02.804
forty-three thousand songs.

147
00:05:03.128 --> 00:05:05.286
They are forty thousand six hundred and

148
00:05:05.286 --> 00:05:05.933
ninety-four.

149
00:05:06.388 --> 00:05:08.746
Two thousand one hundred and sixty-five of

150
00:05:08.746 --> 00:05:11.104
them are stored more than once, byte for

151
00:05:11.104 --> 00:05:12.749
byte, under different names.

152
00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:15.546
Either XMIDI's de-duplication missed them,

153
00:05:15.546 --> 00:05:17.959
or the track merge, described after it,

154
00:05:17.959 --> 00:05:20.208
made different files identical; not

155
00:05:20.208 --> 00:05:20.647
checked.

156
00:05:20.976 --> 00:05:23.389
Mostly pairs; one file under ten names.

157
00:05:23.718 --> 00:05:25.966
One md5 is one song, because the store

158
00:05:25.966 --> 00:05:26.569
keys on it.

159
00:05:26.898 --> 00:05:29.366
And some copies disagree: a hundred and

160
00:05:29.366 --> 00:05:31.450
forty on the genre, six hundred and

161
00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:32.986
seventy-four on the emotion.

162
00:05:33.315 --> 00:05:35.508
Such a song stays in, but in no genre

163
00:05:35.508 --> 00:05:36.989
list, or no emotion list.

164
00:05:38.422 --> 00:05:39.069
Then the build.

165
00:05:39.328 --> 00:05:41.313
The count's rows become the usable list:

166
00:05:41.313 --> 00:05:42.866
one row per song, with its labels.

167
00:05:43.125 --> 00:05:44.679
The clean files are packed into one

168
00:05:44.679 --> 00:05:46.664
archive, each renamed by its md5, because

169
00:05:46.664 --> 00:05:48.433
that is what the store's extractor looks

170
00:05:48.433 --> 00:05:48.605
for.

171
00:05:48.864 --> 00:05:50.892
And the extractor writes the store: forty

172
00:05:50.892 --> 00:05:52.489
thousand six hundred and ninety-four

173
00:05:52.489 --> 00:05:54.474
songs, not one parse failure, thirty-nine

174
00:05:54.474 --> 00:05:56.329
thousand with a bass part, in four and a

175
00:05:56.329 --> 00:05:56.933
third minutes.

176
00:05:57.311 --> 00:05:59.537
Lakh's store took seven and three-quarter

177
00:05:59.537 --> 00:06:01.180
minutes for fifty-two thousand.

178
00:06:01.498 --> 00:06:03.671
The cache the loader reads from, which

179
00:06:03.671 --> 00:06:05.844
lesson two point eight explains, drops

180
00:06:05.844 --> 00:06:06.533
none of them.

181
00:06:08.124 --> 00:06:09.790
Here are the forty thousand songs by

182
00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:10.060
genre.

183
00:06:10.331 --> 00:06:12.222
Country is the biggest, twelve thousand

184
00:06:12.222 --> 00:06:14.429
six hundred; then pop, rock at nine and a

185
00:06:14.429 --> 00:06:16.320
half thousand, and jazz at six thousand.

186
00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.347
Classical and traditional are small,

187
00:06:18.347 --> 00:06:19.743
because few of them have drums.

188
00:06:20.013 --> 00:06:21.724
By emotion, exciting leads with ten

189
00:06:21.724 --> 00:06:23.661
thousand; magnificent has under three

190
00:06:23.661 --> 00:06:24.021
hundred.

191
00:06:24.291 --> 00:06:26.273
Every song has one genre and one emotion,

192
00:06:26.273 --> 00:06:27.534
unless its copies disagreed.

193
00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:32.644
Back to our angry rock file.

194
00:06:32.962 --> 00:06:35.029
Seventy-nine point six three bars, a

195
00:06:35.029 --> 00:06:36.407
hundred and sixty seconds.

196
00:06:36.726 --> 00:06:39.482
Four beats a bar, per second, times sixty:

197
00:06:39.482 --> 00:06:41.602
a hundred and nineteen beats per minute.

198
00:06:42.055 --> 00:06:44.364
When we checked a thousand XMIDI files,

199
00:06:44.364 --> 00:06:46.782
each had been re-encoded the same way:

200
00:06:46.782 --> 00:06:49.200
four hundred and eighty ticks a beat, one

201
00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.619
time signature, one tempo event, and at

202
00:06:51.619 --> 00:06:53.817
least eight in ten of them at a hundred

203
00:06:53.817 --> 00:06:54.532
and nineteen.

204
00:06:54.861 --> 00:06:57.225
Across the whole store, every song has a

205
00:06:57.225 --> 00:06:59.093
single tempo from start to finish.

206
00:06:59.423 --> 00:07:01.402
Thirty-six thousand six hundred and

207
00:07:01.402 --> 00:07:03.435
eighty-one songs, ninety point one

208
00:07:03.435 --> 00:07:05.689
percent, are at a hundred and nineteen.

209
00:07:06.019 --> 00:07:08.327
The rest each carry one other fixed tempo.

210
00:07:08.657 --> 00:07:10.416
The songs' real tempos are lost.

211
00:07:11.839 --> 00:07:14.536
On one tempo axis: Lakh's songs keep their

212
00:07:14.536 --> 00:07:17.232
real tempo maps; the middle eighty percent

213
00:07:17.232 --> 00:07:19.592
of its windows run from eighty-three to a

214
00:07:19.592 --> 00:07:21.839
hundred and sixty-four beats per minute.

215
00:07:22.176 --> 00:07:24.367
XMIDI is one spike, at a hundred and

216
00:07:24.367 --> 00:07:24.872
nineteen.

217
00:07:26.163 --> 00:07:28.316
The model hears the tempo as its fifth

218
00:07:28.316 --> 00:07:29.199
input, x four.

219
00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:31.738
It's the tempo divided by a hundred and

220
00:07:31.738 --> 00:07:32.842
twenty, minus one.

221
00:07:33.343 --> 00:07:34.104
Why that shape?

222
00:07:34.409 --> 00:07:36.440
It's zero at a hundred and twenty and a

223
00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.471
half either side at sixty and a hundred

224
00:07:38.471 --> 00:07:40.705
and eighty, so it stays small, like the

225
00:07:40.705 --> 00:07:41.366
other inputs.

226
00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:43.854
On Lakh it ranges from about minus a third

227
00:07:43.854 --> 00:07:44.666
to plus a third.

228
00:07:44.971 --> 00:07:47.053
On XMIDI, nine songs in ten read minus

229
00:07:47.053 --> 00:07:49.084
zero point zero zero eight: almost

230
00:07:49.084 --> 00:07:50.658
nothing, and always the same.

231
00:07:51.113 --> 00:07:52.330
So what does that mean?

232
00:07:52.648 --> 00:07:54.871
A model can't learn what an input does if

233
00:07:54.871 --> 00:07:56.141
the input never changes.

234
00:07:56.458 --> 00:07:58.575
The genre drummers haven't learned what

235
00:07:58.575 --> 00:07:59.157
tempo does.

236
00:07:59.475 --> 00:08:01.856
Live, the laptop tells them the tempo, a

237
00:08:01.856 --> 00:08:04.132
hundred by default, minus zero point one

238
00:08:04.132 --> 00:08:04.449
seven.

239
00:08:04.767 --> 00:08:06.989
Only one XMIDI window in eighty is within

240
00:08:06.989 --> 00:08:08.577
five beats per minute of that.

241
00:08:08.894 --> 00:08:10.905
On Sena's replays, setting the Rock

242
00:08:10.905 --> 00:08:12.969
drummer's tempo input to a hundred and

243
00:08:12.969 --> 00:08:15.086
nineteen changed nothing, so it isn't

244
00:08:15.086 --> 00:08:15.827
steering them.

245
00:08:16.144 --> 00:08:18.685
For the report: say plainly that the tempo

246
00:08:18.685 --> 00:08:20.378
input is near-constant on XMIDI.

247
00:08:20.696 --> 00:08:22.918
The grid itself is counted in beats, so

248
00:08:22.918 --> 00:08:25.247
it's untouched; lesson two point three

249
00:08:25.247 --> 00:08:25.776
shows why.

250
00:08:29.257 --> 00:08:31.244
Last, the feel, measured over every song

251
00:08:31.244 --> 00:08:31.906
in both stores.

252
00:08:32.171 --> 00:08:34.069
At the median, XMIDI's drums sit tighter

253
00:08:34.069 --> 00:08:36.056
on the sixteenth grid: an offset of two

254
00:08:36.056 --> 00:08:38.043
thousandths of a sixteenth, against eleven

255
00:08:38.043 --> 00:08:38.971
thousandths for Lakh.

256
00:08:39.236 --> 00:08:41.090
But the loosest tenth of XMIDI songs is a

257
00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:42.238
little looser than Lakh's.

258
00:08:42.503 --> 00:08:44.313
Thirty-seven percent of XMIDI songs land

259
00:08:44.313 --> 00:08:45.991
every drum hit exactly on the grid,

260
00:08:45.991 --> 00:08:46.874
against twenty-four.

261
00:08:47.139 --> 00:08:49.038
Lesson two point three covers the grid and

262
00:08:49.038 --> 00:08:49.612
micro-timing.

263
00:08:50.065 --> 00:08:52.517
Velocity, how hard each note is hit, is

264
00:08:52.517 --> 00:08:55.191
not flat: a median of twenty-two different

265
00:08:55.191 --> 00:08:57.196
drum velocities per song, against

266
00:08:57.196 --> 00:08:59.703
twenty-seven, and about one song in eleven

267
00:08:59.703 --> 00:09:02.043
with two or fewer, against one in eight

268
00:09:02.043 --> 00:09:02.544
for Lakh.

269
00:09:02.879 --> 00:09:05.274
The guitar's velocity varies in eighty-six

270
00:09:05.274 --> 00:09:06.221
percent of songs.

271
00:09:06.665 --> 00:09:09.316
